========================================
Z-INDEX IN CSS
========================================

DEFINITION:

z-index controls the stacking order of elements
along the Z-axis (front and back direction).

It decides which element appears on top when
elements overlap.


----------------------------------------
UNDERSTANDING THE Z-AXIS
----------------------------------------

X-axis -> left and right
Y-axis -> top and bottom
Z-axis -> front and back (layering)

z-index works only when elements overlap.


----------------------------------------
HOW Z-INDEX WORKS
----------------------------------------

Higher z-index value  -> appears on top
Lower z-index value   -> appears behind

Example:
z-index: 10;  appears above
z-index: 1;   appears below


----------------------------------------
SYNTAX
----------------------------------------

selector {
    position: relative;   /* or absolute, fixed, sticky */
    z-index: 1;
}


----------------------------------------
VERY IMPORTANT RULE
----------------------------------------

z-index works ONLY on positioned elements.

Position must NOT be:
    position: static;

Position must be one of the following:
    relative
    absolute
    fixed
    sticky


----------------------------------------
DEFAULT VALUE
----------------------------------------

z-index: auto;

If no z-index is specified:
- Elements stack based on HTML order.
- The element written later in HTML appears on top.


----------------------------------------
NEGATIVE Z-INDEX
----------------------------------------

z-index: -1;

Moves the element behind other elements.

Important:
It can go behind its parent background.


----------------------------------------
KEY IMPORTANT POINTS
----------------------------------------

- Controls stacking order (front/back)
- Works only on positioned elements
- Higher value = appears above
- Lower value = appears below
- Default value is auto
- Only visible when elements overlap


----------------------------------------
REAL-WORLD USAGE
----------------------------------------

- Modals
- Dropdown menus
- Fixed navigation bars
- Tooltips
- Image overlays
- Overlapping cards


----------------------------------------
COMMON MISTAKES
----------------------------------------

- Using z-index without setting position
- Using very large numbers unnecessarily
- Not understanding stacking context


----------------------------------------
INTERVIEW ANSWER
----------------------------------------

z-index controls the stacking order of positioned
elements along the Z-axis. Higher values appear
above lower values when elements overlap.


----------------------------------------
SUMMARY
----------------------------------------

- Controls front-back layering
- Works only with non-static positioning
- Higher value appears on top
- Default value is auto
